# Data Sources

The data source supplies rows to the table. It owns the network/IO layer; the table owns the UI. Pick the implementation that matches where the data lives, or implement `IDataSource` for anything custom.

The data source is passed as the `dataSource` prop on `<DataTable>`, separately from `config`.

## LocalDataSource — in-memory arrays

For client-side data you already have in JS. Pagination, sort and search all happen in the browser.

```typescript
import { LocalDataSource } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable';

const dataSource = new LocalDataSource(rows, {
    filtering: {
        textSearchColumns: ['userName', 'mailAddress'],
        filterFunction: (item, query) => /* return true to keep */ true
    }
});
```

- `textSearchColumns` — keys the basic search inspects (case-insensitive substring).
- `filterFunction` — runs after text search. Receives the row and the [`ParsedSearchQuery`](/api-reference/searchParser/interfaces/ParsedSearchQuery), so it can branch on `query.searchFilters` from an [`AdvancedSearchParser`](/docs/guides/search).

<ExampleTable path='daisy-ui/basic/first-example' description='LocalDataSource with text search on userName.' />

## FetchApiDataSource — POST to an endpoint

POSTs a JSON [`PaginatedListRequest`](/api-reference/index/interfaces/PaginatedListRequest) to your URL and expects a [`PaginatedListResponse`](/api-reference/index/interfaces/PaginatedListResponse) back. Aborts in-flight requests when the user changes page/search.

```typescript
import { FetchApiDataSource } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable';

const dataSource = new FetchApiDataSource<UserData>('/api/users/list', {
    credentials: 'include',
    headers: { 'X-CSRF': csrfToken }
});
```

The second argument is forwarded to `fetch` — use it for auth headers, credentials, custom `signal`s, etc.

## ApiFunctionDataSource — wrap any async function

When the request shape doesn't match a single POST (GraphQL, RPC client, third-party SDK), pass an [`ApiFunction`](/api-reference/index/type-aliases/ApiFunction) that maps `PaginatedListRequest` → `Promise<PaginatedListResponse>`.

```typescript
import { ApiFunctionDataSource } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable';

const dataSource = new ApiFunctionDataSource<UserData>(async (request) => {
    const result = await usersClient.list({
        offset: request.start,
        limit: request.amount,
        sort: request.orderBy,
        q: request.searchQuery?.searchText
    });

    return { totalCount: result.total, items: result.rows };
});
```

Throw to surface an error — `onError` and the `errorState` snippet pick it up.

## SvelteQueryDataSource — TanStack Query integration

Same `ApiFunction` shape as above, but cached and refetched through [@tanstack/svelte-query](https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/svelte/overview). The query key is `["dataTable-" + config.type, request]` and is regenerated whenever the request changes.

```typescript
import { SvelteQueryDataSource } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable';

const dataSource = new SvelteQueryDataSource<UserData>(
    (request) => fetchUsers(request),
    { staleTime: 30_000, gcTime: 5 * 60_000 }
);
```

The second argument is forwarded to `useQuery`. Use `setApiFunction`, `setEnabled`, or `updateQueryOptions` to swap behaviour at runtime without re-mounting the table.

<ExampleTable path='daisy-ui/svelte-query' description='SvelteQueryDataSource fetching a static JSON file through TanStack Query.' />

## Custom data source

Implement `IDataSource` for anything else. Two paths:

1. Extend `AbstractDataSource<Data>` and only implement `requestData` — it gives you `queryResult` plumbing for free.
2. Implement `IDataSource` directly when you need full control over the result store.

```typescript
import { AbstractDataSource, QueryResult } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable';
import type { PaginatedListRequest } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable';

class WebsocketDataSource<Data> extends AbstractDataSource<Data> {
    requestData(request: PaginatedListRequest<Data>): void {
        socket.send({ type: 'list', request });
        socket.onMessage = (response) => {
            this.queryResult = QueryResult.buildSuccess(response);
        };
    }
}
```

Implement `fetchOnce` as well if you want **local export** to work — the popover calls it to stream every page without disturbing the visible rows. Skip it and the popover falls back to remote-only export.

